{"availability":"available","full":"abhiramm7/openfloodhub","name":"openfloodhub","description":"Open-source, self-hostable flood-forecasting model — a free alternative to Google Flood Hub. Per-gauge 1D CNN with pretrained checkpoints.","category":"Other","language":"Python","stars":25,"forks":2,"created":"2026-06-18T16:40:22Z","updated":"2026-10-07T02:47:00Z","license":"Apache-2.0","branch":"main","homepage":"https://abhiramm7.github.io/openfloodhub/","topics":[],"demo":"https://abhiramm7.github.io/openfloodhub/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-07T02:54:24.542Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-07T02:54:24.542Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-07T02:54:24.542Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-07T02:54:24.542Z","stars":25,"forks":2}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["An open-source, self-hostable alternative to Google Flood Hub.","Google Flood Hub runs one global model: a single neural network, trained across thousands of basins, that predicts streamflow everywhere from one set of weights. OpenFloodHub does the opposite. It trains a separate small model for each river gauge, on that gauge's own history.","That tradeoff is the whole point of the project:"],"features":["A model that only has to learn one catchment can be tiny (about 50k parameters), train in 90 seconds on a laptop CPU, and you can actually inspect what it learned for that specific river.","A global model generalizes to places with no gauge at all, which a per-site model cannot do. You need a few years of record for each gauge before you can train it."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/abhiramm7/openfloodhub/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"157b551e36535beabaae1ec660ca31bd80b5c3703ef22b1153f87ca35132ea1b","extractedAt":"2026-10-07T02:54:24.703Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-07T02:54:24.703Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"An open-source, self-hostable alternative to Google Flood Hub.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/abhiram